\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Wuliwuli (Wulli Wulli; ISO 639-3 wlu; Glottocode wuli1242) is an extinct Pama-Nyungan language of Queensland, Australia. It was spoken around Eidsvold and the Auburn River catchment, in what is now the North Burnett Regional Council area. It is regarded as closely related to, or a dialect of, Wakka Wakka. The name comes from the pronoun \emph{wuli}, ``they''. The surviving record is fragmentary.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No school materials or programmes were found in the searches.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Wulli Wulli language. It reports that John Mathew published material collected from informants in 1926, that W. E. Parry-Okenden compiled a word list in 1914 at Hawkwood Station, and that Nils Holmer analysed the salvaged data: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wulli_Wulli_language}
\item AIATSIS Austlang record: \url{https://collection.aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/e89}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None were found in the searches.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item State Library of Queensland, Word of the Week (2019 International Year of Indigenous Languages), week thirty-nine. I did not check whether this entry covers Wulli Wulli: \url{https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/2019-international-year-indigenous-languages-word-week-week-thirty-nine}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language has no speakers, so a heritage and revitalization curriculum would depend on the old word lists, AIATSIS holdings and the Wakka Wakka materials. It should be developed with the relevant Aboriginal community and language custodians. I found no existing programme.

\end{document}
